# Glue Job Optimization (1TB → 30 mins) — Clean Version (No Code Blocks)

---

## What interviewer is really asking

They want to see if you can **reduce runtime using engineering decisions**, not just scaling hardware.

Core expectation:

* Diagnose bottlenecks
* Fix Spark execution plan
* Optimize S3 reads/writes
* Then scale DPUs

---

## Simple explanation

Your job is slow because:

* Only 1 partition → no parallelism
* Reading too much data
* Heavy shuffle during groupBy
* Too many small output files
* Possible skew in keys

---

## Correct optimization flow (very important)

Never start with DPU increase.

Correct order:

1. Fix data scan
2. Fix partitioning
3. Fix shuffle
4. Fix skew
5. Optimize output
6. THEN scale DPUs

---

## Step 1 — Fix Parallelism (your biggest issue)

Right now it says “single partition processing”

That means:

* Only one executor is doing work
* Remaining DPUs are idle

Fix by increasing partitions during read or immediately after read:

df = spark.read.parquet("s3://bucket/data/")
df = df.repartition(400)

Why 400?

* Roughly align with cores available
* Avoid underutilization

---

## Step 2 — Partition Pruning (massive impact)

Instead of reading full data:

df = spark.read.parquet("s3://bucket/data/")

Read only required partitions:

df = spark.read.parquet("s3://bucket/data/year=2024/month=03/")

Impact:

* 1TB → maybe 50–100GB
* This alone can reduce runtime drastically

---

## Step 3 — Filter Early (reduce shuffle load)

filtered_df = df.filter(df.status == "active")

Why:

* Reduces data BEFORE aggregation
* Less network + less memory

---

## Step 4 — Enable AQE (Spark intelligence layer)

spark.conf.set("spark.sql.adaptive.enabled","true")
spark.conf.set("spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.enabled","true")
spark.conf.set("spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled","true")

What happens internally:

* Spark adjusts partitions dynamically
* Detects skew and splits large partitions
* Reduces unnecessary shuffle

---

## Step 5 — Fix Data Skew (real-world killer)

Problem:

Some customer_id values may dominate.

Example:

customer_id = NULL or popular → 30% of data

Fix using salting:

df = df.withColumn("salt", (rand()*10).cast("int"))

Then group using both keys:

groupBy(customer_id, salt)

Why:

* Breaks large partitions into smaller ones
* Prevents one executor from becoming bottleneck

---

## Step 6 — Optimize Shuffle

Default shuffle partitions may not be optimal.

spark.conf.set("spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","300")

Rule:

* Too low → large partitions → slow
* Too high → too many tasks → overhead

---

## Step 7 — Output Optimization (very important)

Instead of writing thousands of files:

final_df = transformed_df.coalesce(50)

Why:

* Reduces small files problem
* Improves S3 performance
* Faster downstream queries

---

## Step 8 — Write Partitioned Data

final_df.write.partitionBy("date")

Why:

* Future queries scan less data
* Enables partition pruning

---

## Step 9 — Now Scale DPU (last step)

After all optimizations:

Move from:

10 DPU → 50–100 DPU

Why now?

* Work is parallelized properly
* Scaling now actually helps

---

